+#Continuum: every EEPROM and J-Cart cartridge dump in MAME's software list (hash/megadriv.xml,
+#CC0 data) that this database did not list by checksum. Each is keyed by the sha1 MAME gives for
+#that dump, so it is found whatever product code its header carries. The save hardware is the
+#board MAME gives the set (the comment in each names the set); the wiring and the EEPROM size
+#are this database's own entry for the same game, so a dump it already found by product code
+#behaves exactly as before, or, for a game it did not have, its entry for a game MAME puts on
+#the same board. The last one is the opposite case: a Rockman Mega World with SRAM, not the
+#EEPROM the other one has, which the product code entry would otherwise give it.
+eb4aca22f8b5837a0a0b10491c46714948b09844 {
+	#evander: Evander Holyfield's 'Real Deal' Boxing (World)
+	name Evander 'Real Deal' Holyfield's Boxing
+	EEPROM {
+		type i2c
+		size 128
+	}
+	map {
+		0 {
+			device ROM
+			last 1FFFFF
+		}
+		200000 {
+			device EEPROM
+			last 3FFFFF
+			bits_read {
+				0 sda
+			}
+			bits_write {
+				0 sda
+				1 scl
+			}
+		}
+	}
+}
